{# SNMP, the one telemetry path this firewall can be given through the model
   (cycle 034). gNMI/JTI lives under `system services extension-service`, which
   the push path refuses by design.

   Conditional like `applications`: with no community modelled nothing renders,
   which is what keeps the artifact byte-identical to a device file that has no
   stanza. The push is a full `load override`, so removing it from the model
   removes it from the device.

   THE COMMUNITY IS BOUND TO `mgmt_junos`, because fxp0 sits in that instance
   under `management-instance` and every poll arrives there. Unbound -- or
   bound with the wrong `clients` -- Junos counts each request as a "Bad
   community use" and answers nothing. `routing-instance-access` lets the agent
   accept requests in a non-default instance at all.

   The clients are the MANAGEMENT NETWORK, not vrnetlab's internal 10.0.0.0/24:
   vrnetlab forwards UDP/161 with the poller's source address intact, so fxp0
   sees 172.20.41.x -- the host, or a k3s node masquerading the Telegraf pod.
   Measured with `monitor traffic interface fxp0`, then answered `sysName=fw1`
   with exactly this stanza. #}
{% if snmp %}
snmp {
    /* Read-only, polled by the telemetry collector the monitoring profiles
     * configure. A v2c community has to be in the configuration the device
     * receives; it is restricted to the one client prefix below. */
    community {{ snmp.community }} {
        authorization read-only;
        routing-instance mgmt_junos {
            clients {
                {{ snmp.clients }};
            }
        }
    }
    routing-instance-access;
}
{% endif %}
